  "live_records": {
    "q-44539": {
      "dom_id": "step-q44539",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,4)$ and is parallel to $3x-y=7.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $3x-y=7.$ \n\nTo find the slope of the line $3x-y=7,$ we convert the equation to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y & =7 \\\\ y & =3x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept form, we can tell that the slope of the line is $3.$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $3$ that passes through the point $(6,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-6) \\\\ y-4 & =3x-18 \\\\ y & =3x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-14 \\\\ -3x+y & =-14 \\\\ 3x-y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
